            <ArticleTitle>South Asian Health: Inflammation, Infection, Exposure, and the Human Microbiome.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s10903-017-0652-y</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This paper presents the results of the literature review conducted for the working group topic on inflammation, infection, exposure, and the human microbiome. Infection and chronic inflammation can elevate risk for cardiovascular disease and cancer. Environmental exposures common among South Asian (SA) subgroups, such as arsenic exposure among Bangladeshis and particulate matter air pollution among taxi drivers, also pose risks. This review explores the effects of exposure to arsenic and particulate matter, as well as other infections common among SAs, including human papillomavirus (HPV) and hepatitis B/C infection. Emerging research on the human microbiome, and the effect of microbiome changes on obesity and diabetes risk among SAs are also explored.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
